<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Find the coordinates of the two stationary points of the curve <m>y = {x^3+3 x^2-9 x+4}</m>. Give the point with the smaller <m>x</m>-coordinate first.</p><div>First point: ( <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/> , <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> )   </div><div>Second point: ( <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/> , <fillin characters="5" name="ans4"/> )   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>At a stationary point the tangent is horizontal: the curve is momentarily neither rising nor falling, so <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 0</m>.</p><p><me>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {3 x^2+6 x-9} = {3 \left(x-1\right) \left(x+3\right)}</me>This is zero when <m>x = {-3}</m> or <m>x = {1}</m>.</p><p>When <m>x = {-3}</m>, <m>y = {31}</m>. When <m>x = {1}</m>, <m>y = {-1}</m>.</p><p>The stationary points are <m>({-3}, {31})</m> and <m>({1}, {-1})</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>-3</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>31</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-1</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>